{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And YHWH spoke to Moses in the wilderness of Sinai, in the tent of meeting, saying.</b> And in the portion of וַיִּקְרָא it is written: ״מֵאֹהֶל מוֹעֵד״ (from the tent of meeting), because at that time Moses could not enter the tent of meeting since the cloud settled upon it; therefore the call came to him from the tent of meeting to outside the tent of meeting. But on the first of Iyyar, Moses was already able to enter inside the tent, and the divine speech came to him in the tent of meeting from above the cover, as is found at the end of the portion of נָשֹׂא. And there is no need for what Rashi explained there — 'the third one came and decided,' and so forth. And the reason it specified ״בְּאֹהֶל מוֹעֵד״ (in the tent of meeting) is to give a reason for this counting: for it was because He came to cause His Shechinah to dwell among them. The language of לְהַשְׁרוֹת (to cause to dwell) denotes the establishment of the Shechinah as a permanent dwelling there, and a permanent dwelling is not in less than thirty days. Therefore on the first of Nisan the Tabernacle was erected, and on that very day the Shechinah descended into the tent of meeting; and after thirty days, when it was seen that YHWH had made for Himself a dwelling among the lower realms as a permanent dwelling, He counted them on the first of Iyyar. And He did not count them immediately on the first of Nisan, because it had not yet held firm in this dwelling." "And it appears to me to give a reason for this counting at the time of the indwelling of the Shechinah, for the Sages, blessed be their memory, said: the Holy One, blessed be He, does not cause His Shechinah to rest upon Israel with fewer than twenty-two thousand, as it is said (Numbers 10:36): ״וּבְנֻחֹה יֹאמַר שׁוּבָה ה׳ רִבְבוֹת אַלְפֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״. And the reason for this matter is to establish His kingdom, blessed be He, on earth in the manner of the kingdom of heaven, as the Sages said (Tanchuma, Vayishlach 2): at the time the Holy One, blessed be He, revealed Himself to Israel at Mount Sinai, twenty-two thousand chariots of ministering angels descended with Him, as it is said (Psalms 68:18): ״רֶכֶב אֱלֹהִים רִבֹּתַיִם אַלְפֵי שִׁנְאָן ה׳ בָּם״ — which means to say, when there is this number, then ״ה׳ בָּם״, the Shechinah dwells among them. And so we find regarding the camp of Leviyyim, which was called the camp of the Shechinah, that they were twenty-two thousand (Numbers 3:39). Therefore it was necessary to count Israel as well, to know whether among them there was this number, so that they would be fit for the indwelling of the Shechinah in each and every tribe." "And Israel required a counting of exactly sixty myriad, no less, because Rabbeinu Bachye wrote on this portion (Numbers 2:2) that in every place where the Shechinah is present there you will find four camps of the Shechinah. And in Sefer ha-Pardes, Sha'ar Pratei ha-Shemot, chapter 2, page 118, he wrote that there are twenty-eight camps of the Shechinah. And from where does he learn to say this? I say that he learned this from the number that results from the indwelling of the Shechinah in the encompassing of seven clouds, with its interior densely packed with love in the camp of Leviyyah; and in each and every cloud was the glory of YHWH; and in every place where the glory of YHWH is present, there are no fewer than four camps of the Shechinah, and in each and every camp, twenty-two thousand. It emerges that in seven clouds there are twenty-eight camps — in each cloud, four camps. Go and calculate twenty-eight times twenty-two: you will find six hundred thousand and another sixteen thousand. Thus Israel were six hundred and three thousand, and the Leviyyim twenty-two thousand — a total of six hundred and twenty-five thousand. For in every hundred thousand there are four times twenty-two thousand, and twelve thousand remain; it emerges that there are twenty-four camps in the total of six hundred thousand. Now further calculate from six times twelve thousand: three camps remain, nine thousand, and the Leviyyim twenty-two thousand — a total of twenty-eight camps. And although there is here a surplus of nine thousand, in any case one should not diminish it among the lower realms, for death consumes them and perhaps the number would not be filled from those coming of age at twenty so quickly." "And the Leviyyim were counted from one month old, for that tribe is practiced to be counted from the womb, as is said of Jeremiah the priest: ״וּבְטֶרֶם תֵּצֵא מֵרֶחֶם הִקְדַּשְׁתִּיךָ״ (Jeremiah 1:5). Therefore the matter is compelled: in all the numberings, Israel were not fewer than six hundred thousand, apart from the Leviyyim. Although in the first counting they were six hundred thousand together with the Leviyyim, and they did not amount to twenty-eight camps among the lower realms to encompass seven clouds — in any case, the Holy One, blessed be He, had not yet come to cause His Shechinah to dwell among the lower realms, to make for Himself a permanent dwelling among the lower realms. But now, on the first of Iyyar, after thirty days have established a presumption that His dwelling would be permanent — and Elohim came with all His holy ones to establish a dwelling among the lower realms, as ״תִּפְאֶרֶת אָדָם לָשֶׁבֶת בָּיִת״ (Isaiah 44:13) — from then on it was necessary to count them, to show that they needed to be this number in order to fill twenty-eight camps of twenty-two thousand each, at that time when YHWH established His Shechinah in a permanent dwelling in the tent of meeting. Therefore it says here ״בְּאֹהֶל מוֹעֵד״. And this is a precious interpretation, and it reconciles with the number six hundred thousand, regarding which most of the commentators set out to find a sufficient reason and did not find one." "<b>On the first day of the second month, in the second year.</b> The need for this date written here — they said in Midrash Tanchuma (5): A parable of a king who married a wife and did not write her a ketubah, etc. After some days he found a poor woman of good family and wrote her a ketubah and wrote the date for her, etc. See the text of the midrash in the Yalkut (684). The opinion of the author of this midrash is that the day of the giving of the Torah is the day of betrothal, for the Holy One, blessed be He, betrothed Israel through this Torah, as it is written (Deuteronomy 33:4): ״תּוֹרָה צִוָּה לָנוּ מֹשֶׁה מוֹרָשָׁה קְהִלַּת יַעֲקֹב״, and our Sages, blessed be their memory, expounded (Pesachim 49b): ״מוֹרָשָׁה״ — מְאוֹרָשָׂה (betrothed). And know that ״לִקְהִלַּת יַעֲקֹב״ was not said, but rather ״מְאוֹרָשָׂה קְהִלַּת יַעֲקֹב״ — meaning that the congregation of Jacob is betrothed to the Holy One, blessed be He, through His giving her the Torah in place of a ring of betrothal, as it is written (Isaiah 54:5): ״כִּי בֹעֲלַיִךְ עֹשַׂיִךְ״. And behold, we find that our Sages, blessed be their memory (Tanchuma, Tissa 16), compared the union of Israel with the Holy One, blessed be He, to the union of a bride with a groom at two times: the first is at the time of the giving of the Torah, as it is written (Exodus 31:18): ״וַיִּתֵּן אֶל מֹשֶׁה כְּכַלֹּתוֹ״ — written defectively (without the vav), which teaches that the Torah was handed over like a bride adorned in twenty-four books. The second is on the day of the dedication of the Tabernacle, as it is written (Numbers 7:1): ״וַיְהִי בְּיוֹם כַּלּוֹת מֹשֶׁה לְהָקִים״, which Rashi explained: on the day the Tabernacle was erected, Israel were handed over to the Holy One, blessed be He, like a bride to a groom. Hear from this that the giving of the Torah is the time of betrothal, and the day the Tabernacle was erected is the time of the wedding — ״כִּתְפְאֶרֶת אָדָם לָשֶׁבֶת בַּיִת״ (Isaiah 44:13), as is understood from the language of ״נִמְסְרוּ״ (were handed over), that is, into a single dwelling. And between the one and the other was about ten months, as it is said (Genesis 24:55): ״תֵּשֵׁב הַנַּעֲרָ אִתָּנוּ יָמִים אוֹ עָשׂוֹר״ — and that is the ten months that are given to a virgin as the time between betrothal and the wedding, to adorn herself with the twenty-four kinds of adornment mentioned in Isaiah (3:18–24)." "And in order to liken the mating of Israel to the Holy One, blessed be He, in every respect to an ordinary mating, the Holy One, blessed be He, also limited the time of Israel to ten months — from the first of the month of Sivan, in which they were betrothed through the Torah 'which Moses commanded us,' until the first of the month of Nisan, in which the Mishkan was erected and the mating was completed in every respect. For during this time Israel would become hardened in twenty-four books, and from this they are worthy to dwell in the Tent of Meeting — a single dwelling for them both. And since the time of nuptials is thirty days (see Ketubot 8), therefore the time extended until the first day of the second month in the second year. And if you wish to count from the verse 'And He gave to Moses when He finished' — which was said in the month of Tammuz after forty days that He brought down the tablets — then the count will work out to ten months until the second month of the second year, for then the nuptials were completed, and HaShem wrote her a ketubbah, to know from which time the property of the acquired wife may be seized. For through the ketubbah, the lands of seven nations became enslaved to them, so that if on account of their sin they are exiled from the land, they are able to return and seize property from them immediately and say, 'The date of our deed is earlier.' Therefore it says here 'in the Tent of Meeting,' for this is the time of nuptials, to establish a home. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory mentioned in tractate Ta'anit (26a) these two times, and they expounded: 'On the day of his wedding' — this is the giving of the Torah; 'and on the day of the gladness of his heart' — this is the building of the Holy Temple. For in these two times the mating was completed. And He mentioned 'on the first day of the month' for the reason that has been explained, and so that the mating would be like for like, it says 'Lift up the head of the children of Israel' — according to the view of the Midrash (Numbers Rabbah 1:9), which says that just as it is said of the Holy One, blessed be He, 'and the One who is exalted above all as head' (1 Chronicles 29:11), so too I have given you elevation of head, etc. And what is this matter relevant here? Only so that the mating would be like for like, as it were."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {2 "The arithmetic in the original is elliptical and at points difficult to follow; I have rendered the calculations hyper-literally, preserving the author's compressed logic without smoothing.", 3 "The Aramaic phrase חֲזָקָה (presumption of permanence) is applied to the thirty-day threshold of residence; I have rendered it as 'established a presumption.'", 4 "The citation ישעיה מד יג (Isaiah 44:13) appears in the original and is rendered exactly as written; the phrase כתפארת אדם לשבת בית is drawn from Targum on that verse.", 5 "The word יִתְקַשְּׁטוּ (normally 'they will be strengthened') is rendered hyper-literally as 'they will become hardened' to preserve the ambiguous root; it may carry a textual nuance of becoming firmly established in Torah study."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:40:10.358670698Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}